                 United States & Canada (US / CA) — A2P 10DLC & Toll-Free Verification
The Regulation: Carrier networks in the US and Canada strictly monitor A2P (Application-to-Person) SMS.
How it works (10DLC): If you send SMS from a standard 10-digit long code phone number, you must register your Brand (business identity) and Campaign (what you are sending) with The Campaign Registry (TCR).
Toll-Free Verification: If you send from a Toll-Free number, the number must go through a formal verification process declaring your business and opt-in method.
Why it matters: Unregistered numbers sending A2P messages face nearly 100% block rates by major carriers (T-Mobile, AT&T, Verizon) and suffer high carrier surcharges.
2. India (IN) — DLT (Distributed Ledger Technology) Registry
The Regulation: Regulated by the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) to combat massive SMS spam.
How it works: India uses a blockchain-based registry called DLT. To deliver messages, you must register:
Principal Entity (PE): Your business registration on a carrier's DLT portal.
Sender Headers (Sender IDs): The 6-character alphabetic header indicating who sent the message (e.g., CP-CREDUP).
Content Templates: The exact structure of your messages (e.g., OTP texts, promotional messages). Every template must have variables defined (e.g., Your OTP is {#var#}).
Why it matters: If the message body sent by your application does not exactly match a registered and approved template in the DLT registry, local operators will instantly drop the message.
3. Singapore (SG) — SSIR (SMS Sender ID Registry)
The Regulation: Operated by the Singapore Network Information Centre (SGNIC) to eliminate spoofing and phishing scams.
How it works: Any organization wishing to send SMS using an alphanumeric Sender ID (like CREDUPE) to Singapore mobile numbers must register that specific ID using their Singapore UEN (Unique Entity Number).
Why it matters: Any unregistered alphanumeric Sender ID will be automatically overwritten and labeled by carriers as "Likely-SCAM" or completely blocked.
4. Malaysia (MY) — "RM0.00" Prefix & Content Rules
The Regulation: Governed by the Malaysian Communications and Multimedia Commission (MCMC) to protect consumers.
How it works:
"RM0.00" Prefix: Every message body must start with the prefix RM0.00 (Ringgit Malaysia 0.00). This indicates to the consumer that they are not being charged to receive the message.
Link Restrictions: Malaysian carriers heavily filter messages that contain URLs, callback numbers, or requests for personal/financial info.
Why it matters: Messages without the RM0.00 prefix or containing restricted content (like unregistered short-links) are rejected by operators.
5. Indonesia (ID) — Brand Identity & Keyword Filtering
The Regulation: Designed by Indonesian carriers to secure OTP verification paths.
How it works:
Brand Matching: Messages must include your business/brand name inside the message body.
Keyword Restrictions: Indonesian telcos (like Telkomsel and XL Axiata) actively filter and block unregistered traffic containing sensitive OTP keywords like Verify, PIN, OTP, or Code.
Why it matters: To reliably send transactional updates or OTPs, you must whitelist your sender ID and register your OTP templates directly with the carriers through your SMS provider.
6. Germany (DE) — Explicit Opt-In (UWG)
The Regulation: Germany enforces some of the strictest data privacy and anti-spam laws (under GDPR and the German Act Against Unfair Competition - UWG).
How it works: You must have prior, explicit, and documented consent (double opt-in is highly recommended) before sending any marketing messages.
Why it matters: Sending unsolicited SMS in Germany exposes businesses to massive regulatory fines and legal cease-and-desist warnings (Abmahnung) from competitors or consumer rights organizations.
7. France (FR) — Mandated Sending Windows & "STOP" Opt-Out
The Regulation: Regulated by ARCEP and CNIL to protect consumer privacy.
How it works:
Strict Marketing Hours: Promotional and marketing SMS can only be sent between 08:00 and 21:30 (local French time) from Monday to Saturday. Sending promotional SMS on Sundays, French public holidays, or late at night is strictly prohibited.
"STOP" Keyword: Every marketing message must end with a standard opt-out command (e.g., STOP au 36xxx).
Why it matters: French carriers use time-based filters to block promotional messages outside the allowed windows. (Note: Transactional messages like OTP security codes are exempt from the time limits and can be sent 24/7).